  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 501(c)(3), the private benefit doctrine is broader than the private inurement prohibition. Private benefit applies to any person, including outsiders. Paying $300,000 for services worth $80,000 provides impermissible private benefit to the consultant, even though the consultant is not an insider.
Wrong (B): Private inurement applies only to insiders with a personal stake in the organization. The consultant has no governance role or insider connection, so the inurement prohibition does not apply.
Wrong (C): Independent contractor status does not shield from the private benefit doctrine. Any excessive payment to any party can constitute private benefit regardless of employment classification.
Wrong (D): Private benefit and private inurement are distinct doctrines with different scopes. Inurement is limited to insiders; private benefit extends to all persons.$EXPL$
